WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:03.310
Can a licensing rule affect your earnings

00:03.310 --> 00:06.100
even when it applies to a different job?

00:07.240 --> 00:10.380
Imagine two people at drafting benches,

00:10.380 --> 00:12.530
doing the same kind of work.

00:13.040 --> 00:15.890
One lives on this side of a state line.

00:16.170 --> 00:18.610
The other lives just across it.

00:19.110 --> 00:21.040
Their jobs look alike.

00:21.460 --> 00:24.440
Their options for their next job
might differ.

00:25.088 --> 00:28.898
An occupational license is
permission from the state

00:28.898 --> 00:31.108
to work in a particular job.

00:31.728 --> 00:36.018
A state might require an exam
or a period of training

00:36.018 --> 00:37.728
to protect the public.

00:38.338 --> 00:40.808
For someone deciding where to work,

00:40.968 --> 00:43.348
those requirements can also affect

00:43.348 --> 00:45.668
which jobs are within reach.

00:46.054 --> 00:49.454
To study what happens
beyond the licensed job,

00:49.724 --> 00:53.334
economist Sam Dodini first had to work out

00:53.334 --> 00:57.414
which occupations might
be realistic alternatives.

00:58.024 --> 01:01.214
He used a U.S. database called O*NET

01:01.214 --> 01:04.644
to group jobs that rely on similar skills.

01:05.164 --> 01:08.314
Think of work that
calls for reading plans,

01:08.344 --> 01:12.554
measuring accurately, or
using specialised tools.

01:12.984 --> 01:16.954
Those skills can carry over
from one job to another.

01:17.434 --> 01:21.294
Dodini compared people
in the same occupation

01:21.294 --> 01:25.064
who lived near opposite sides
of the same state border.

01:25.824 --> 01:28.724
The border keeps the comparison local,

01:28.874 --> 01:31.304
while state rules can make a difference to

01:31.304 --> 01:34.114
which other jobs require a license.

01:34.534 --> 01:36.664
The study accounts for licensing

01:36.664 --> 01:39.304
in the workers’ own occupation, too.

01:39.704 --> 01:44.274
It makes this comparison across
many occupations and borders

01:44.274 --> 01:47.474
to estimate how licensing in other jobs

01:47.474 --> 01:49.814
affects pay and employment.

01:50.324 --> 01:53.764
Suppose twenty percent
of workers in other jobs

01:53.764 --> 01:57.444
with similar skills need
a license in one state,

01:57.504 --> 01:59.534
and thirty percent in the other.

02:00.264 --> 02:03.124
That’s a ten-percentage-point difference.

02:04.224 --> 02:07.944
In the study, a difference
that size corresponded to

02:07.944 --> 02:11.904
about one point six percent
lower weekly earnings,

02:11.904 --> 02:16.064
on average, for workers
in the occupation being compared.

02:21.040 --> 02:23.790
For that same increase in licensing,

02:24.120 --> 02:28.690
weekly earnings fell by about
two point eight percent for women,

02:28.790 --> 02:31.730
compared with less than
one percent for men.

02:32.690 --> 02:35.220
Across racial and ethnic groups,

02:35.450 --> 02:40.090
non-Hispanic Black workers
had the largest estimated decline.

02:40.730 --> 02:42.480
Among Hispanic workers,

02:42.700 --> 02:45.650
the drop was larger
for those born abroad.

02:46.229 --> 02:49.309
Weekly earnings were
only part of the picture.

02:50.239 --> 02:53.679
Where licensing was more
common in similar jobs,

02:53.759 --> 02:57.969
employment in the occupation
being compared was lower too.

02:58.719 --> 03:01.909
The paper separately
found an earnings premium

03:01.909 --> 03:05.649
when licensing applied
to workers’ own occupation.

03:06.136 --> 03:10.317
One possible explanation has
to do with the options you have

03:10.317 --> 03:12.106
if you want to leave a job.

03:12.837 --> 03:16.206
A new role might use
skills you already have

03:16.247 --> 03:18.467
and still require a license.

03:19.027 --> 03:21.136
If that makes switching harder,

03:21.367 --> 03:25.396
your employer has less reason
to raise your pay to keep you.

03:26.367 --> 03:29.727
The findings are also
consistent with changes in

03:29.727 --> 03:32.807
how many workers employers want to hire.

03:33.269 --> 03:34.669
That’s the spillover.

03:34.669 --> 03:38.209
A licensing rule for one occupation

03:38.209 --> 03:40.629
can change earnings and employment

03:40.629 --> 03:44.119
in other occupations
that use similar skills.

03:44.579 --> 03:47.039
To see who’s affected by a rule,

03:47.199 --> 03:50.199
look at the other jobs
those workers could do.

03:51.270 --> 03:54.060
To learn more, check out the full paper!
